------------------------------------- Resource API ------------------------------------------------

- LOAD THE RACES USING THE RESOURCE API.


1️⃣ We already have a RaceService.list() method to fetch the pending races. Let’s now migrate it to 
    Angular’s resource API.
    🔥httpResource() lets us expose HTTP data as a resource rather than an Observable. So
    RaceService.list() should now return a resource,
    for example a ResourceRef<Array<RaceModel> | undefined>. Update the method accordingly.
    (see section 4)


2️⃣ You should then update the Races component to use the resource directly. 🔥Since RaceService.list()
    returns a resource, the component no longer needs toSignal().


3️⃣ In the Races template, display <div role="status">Loading.</div> while the request is pending.
    Then display <div class="alert alert-danger" role="alert">An error occurred while loading races.
    </div> if the resource is in error.( see section 4 and then section 2)
    Let's also add a bootstrap loading animation to make it look nicer(⚠️note update test code accordinly
    or don't use it and keep it simple):

        <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" disabled>
            <span class="spinner-border spinner-border-sm" role="status" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            Loading...
        </button>


4️⃣ Last step, render the races with races.value() in the @for loop once the resource has loaded.


5️⃣ Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK.